CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20 --unwind 4 --object-bits 12 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ std::function holds a lambda closure and calls it: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*CONVERSION ERROR.*$
^.*address_arithmetic.*$
--
[expr.prim.lambda.closure]/1: a lambda has a unique closure class type; a
captureless, non-generic lambda is now lowered to a closure struct with an
operator() (the body) and a non-explicit conversion to pointer-to-function
(§7.5.6.2).  The lambda expression is a closure object, so std::function stores
it by value and the type-erased call invokes its operator() correctly.  Phase A
of doc/architectural/cpp-lambda-closure-support.md.
